A||N||13||Eriamjh||How to remove Ctrl Arm Pivot Bolts?||||1||
Z||000000||Eriamjh||11-20-2001||06:10 PM||||Does anyone know how to get the pivot bolts out of the upper and lower control arms on an '87?  My friend says he passed on a bizzarre tool that  he now realizes was for pushing out these bolts.  Naturally, this was a flea market thing and he can't go back.<P>I don't have the room to hammer them out.  No room for a drift or to swing the hammer.  The bottoms may come out with a c-clamp, but I'm not holding my breath.<P>Can I get this tool at a typical automotive store?  Does anyone have any suggestions without buying special "buy it to do this one job and never need it again" tools?  Has anyone ever done this?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Nashco||11-20-2001||07:06 PM||||I feel for you man, I went through hell getting them out!  Here's how it went down:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/013842.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/013842.html</A> <P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||PBJ||11-20-2001||07:56 PM||||AHHH, The joys of an air hammer.<p>[This message has been edited by PBJ (edited 11-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Eriamjh||11-20-2001||08:38 PM||||It seems that the rear inner sleeve is siezed to the upper control arm pivot bolt.  It's preventing the thing from sliding out of the tube.<P><B>Nashco:</B> I read your thread.  I seem to be having the same troubles except I'm not trying to do this in a weekend.  Are you a GMI/Kettering student by chance?  I don't know any other reason one would live in Flint, MI.  I'm class of '92.<P>I have tried a little burning of the bush-ing (note the near-religious reference) and it's stinky and messy.  I will have to try splitting the inner sleeve to get it to break loose.<P>I will also have to look into new bolts because cutting the heads off and driving them through to the other side seems to be the easiest thing to do.  Other than grade 10.9, is there anything else I need to know when I call around for a good bolt supplier?  What is the bolt diameter?||||reg||8
Z||000004||PBJ||11-20-2001||09:38 PM||||Price out the bolts before you cut them.  Long, strong bolts aren't cheap. To pay a garage to do this job should only be 2-3 hrs. If your going poly, thats even less work. It takes me less than an hour to do both sides at work, and you don't have to have and alignment after.  I agree you are on the correct track when you are splitting the inner collar.  I have also used a pickle fork between head of bolt and upper control arm to aid in the process.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Nashco||11-20-2001||10:52 PM||||You got it, I do go to Kettering; just like you said, who the hell else lives there on purpose? (No offense Tigger <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)  If things go alright, I'll probably be class of '05.<P>If you look at that link that I gave you up  top, you'll see that Tigger (Brent) told me where to get the hardware there, at Mid-State Bolt and Screw.  That's a ways away for you, but if you can't find a place around there that has them, they have everything.  It cost about nine bucks to get four bolts and four nuts, grade 10.9.  The bolt size, I believe, was 90mm x 12mm x 1.5mm<P>The stock thread pitch was 1.25mm I believe, but the 1.5mm was cheaper (about 25% as much).  Obviously, thread pitch didn't matter as long as the bolt filled the hole <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>One thing to do when calling for hardware is make sure you can get a locking nut.  I haven't been able to get locking nuts yet (forgot to first time around..DOH) and it's a constant worry for me.<P>Also, make sure you put some sort of anti-sieze or grease on everything when you put it together.<P>Good luck splitting the sleeve.  As you read, I tried but it didn't get me anywhere.  If that works, it should be a hell of a lot easier than cutting the bolts and bending the frame tabs.<P>Have fun! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eriamjh||11-21-2001||08:25 AM||||Thanks for the bolt info.  I'm sure I can find a bolt place in the Detroit area that has 'em.<P>If you can't find locking nuts and you are worried about it coming loose, put two nuts on the bolt end.  Tighten the second one against the first.  That should create a "thread bind" that prevents either one from loosening.<P>Well, I'm off to keep at this thing.  It's too late to take it to a shop.  The car is suspensionless in my garage.<P>Here's another question:  Did you press the poly bushings in yourself or have a shop do it?  How hard was it?||||reg||4
Z||000007||neohio||11-21-2001||08:53 AM||||Order the upper control arm bolts from the fiero store..you'll play hell trying to find them at a hardware store..the part number for the uppers is..57017 the lower ones shouldn't be a problem||||reg||1
Z||000008||Nashco||11-21-2001||10:13 AM||||Ha, if it were only as easy as pulling extra nuts from thin air!  That's the thing, I haven't made time to find a nut and bolt store (I'm in 'bama now).<P>Installing the poly is easy.  Removing the rubber is the worst part.  Lots of people say to use a torch and burn it, and lots of people also say to press it out.  Some even say to cut or drill the rubber out (no thank you).  I made a happy compromise; I used a torch to heat the outside of the outer sleeve until the bushings just started to crackle and smoke.  Then, very quickly, I pressed the rubber out while the outer edges were liquified.  This took about 20 minutes to do all eight rubber bushings, uppers and lowers.<P>After heating and pressing the bushings out, I used a dremel to clean all of the leftover rubber out.  This took about another hour.<P>You should have a good smooth, clean surface after all that, and it should only take you a few hours after you have the arms off the car.  Make sure you use lots of the teflon grease, and the part that is most important to grease, I've been told, is between the outer sleeve and the poly.<P>It's really easy to get in.  It goes in about halfway before it gets tough.  I just used a 1.5 pound rubber mallet and they all went in fine.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000009||kinboyatuwo||11-22-2001||01:52 AM||||PBJ just did mine<P>I nominate him member of the month<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/b4lower50.jpg"> <BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."<P>87 Duke, 235,000km||||reg||1
Z||000010||Eriamjh||11-22-2001||01:27 PM||||Well, I got the upper ctrl arm bolt out.  It was indeed siezed to the bushing sleeve on the bolt side.  Splitting it slightly was all it took to break it free.<P>However, the same seems to be true of the lowers and because of the frame, I don't have the same access as the top.  One down, five to go.  I'm using the extra time it take to soak down the other side with tons of penetrant.  Maybe I'll only have one side to f*ck with.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Eriamjh||11-24-2001||11:28 AM||||ARGH!<P>It's day 7 and I only have one ot the six control arm bolts out!<P>The lowers are seized so bad I bent a huge C-Clamp trying to press one out.  Heat and soaking don't affect it at all.<P>Is my only recourse cutting the heads off with a grinder and punching out the bolts with an air hammer?<P>This really sucks so far.  <P>------------------<BR>Dan||||reg||1
Z||000012||Eriamjh||11-25-2001||08:18 PM||||Well, Nashco was right.  The grinder was the way to go.<P>I rented on from Home Depot for $15 and bought 5 "metal" grinding discs, one for each bolt I had to do.<P>To make it short, it worked like a charm.  I completely removed the head of the front lower control arm bolt and then drove it through to the rear with my air hammer!  The air hammer was still a b*tch to punch out those studs, but it worked.  Took probably 2.5 hours to get all 5 remaining bolts out.<P>Ironically, the rears just plain turned out.  I just kept turning and turning them and they worked their way out.<P>I have never seen pivot bolts as corroded and f*cked over as these were.  A good .020" of an inch has disappeared from each bolt due to rusting against the sleeve.  GM must have used non-plated sleeves or cheap bolts or something crappy.<P>I'm heading to Metro Bolt and Fastener tomorrow to get some grade 10.9 or better 12mm bolts to replace them all.  These will get greased up before it all gets assembled.<P>Thanks everyone for your help!  Now, the adventure can continue...||||reg||1
Z||000013||PBJ||11-25-2001||08:41 PM||||GM certianly was not thinking about service-ability down the road, ehh.||||reg||1
